# Shared rules for the Hermes bot team (NOT the Cursor editor)

This file happens to share its name with the "Cursor" code editor's own
auto-loaded rules file — pure coincidence of Hermes' own convention (it
auto-injects AGENTS.md, SOUL.md, and this file as "rules" for every bot
profile pointed at this repo, same mechanism, different tool). If Cursor is
ever used on this repo too, it would also read this file; the rules below
are harmless bot etiquette, not harmful to a human editor session, but this
file is written for the Hermes bot team specifically.

Applies to every Hermes bot profile working in this repo, in addition to
that profile's own SOUL.md and this repo's AGENTS.md.

## Headless mode has no live messaging

Bots normally run headless (`hermes -p <profile> -z "..."`), where no
running Hermes gateway is available. Hermes' own `message_agent`/`@-mention`
delivery does NOT reach anyone in that mode — not another bot, not Alex.
- Anything meant for Alex goes directly in your own final response text,
  never behind an `@user` mention.
- Anything meant for another bot: if your own SOUL.md gives you terminal
  access and an explicit hand-off step, use a direct CLI call to that
  profile (`hermes -p <profile> -z "..."`) instead of `@name`. If you have
  no terminal access, note the hand-off in plain text in whatever you write
  (an issue body, a file) for a human or a later bot run to pick up — not as
  a live message.

## Never expose real training/wellness data

Never read or quote `data/*.json` (real synced training/wellness data for
real athletes, including health-adjacent values like HRV/resting heart
rate) unless the task explicitly requires it and Alex has confirmed it for
that specific task.

## Always name the repo explicitly

On every `gh`/script call that touches GitHub, pass the repo explicitly
(`-R Stuhlsen/training-dashboard` or equivalent). Never rely on your
current shell/terminal directory to imply which repo you mean, even if you
believe it is already correct — a terminal session can be long-lived and
may have drifted from an earlier task.

## Output language

Everything that leaves your own session — GitHub content (issue titles,
bodies, comments, PR text), files you write, chat summaries — stays
English, regardless of what language the prompt or instruction you were
given used. This repo's convention is English-only tracked content (see
AGENTS.md); a German instruction should not pull the output toward German.
